<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>

<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name      "ports">
<!ENTITY author    "poozer">
<!ENTITY version   "2026.07.23.01">
<!ENTITY md5       "dbfc52f4d248f1a2ae9721aec1507673">
<!ENTITY pkg       "&name;-&version;-noarch-1">
<!ENTITY launch    "Settings/Ports">
<!ENTITY repo      "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/marching-poozer/unraid-port-viewer/main">
<!ENTITY plgPATH   "/boot/config/plugins/&name;">
<!ENTITY emhttp    "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;">
]>

<PLUGIN name="&name;" author="&author;" version="&version;" launch="&launch;"
        pluginURL="&repo;/ports.plg"
        icon="ports.png"
        min="7.0.0"
        comment="A scannable view of every TCP/UDP listener with its owning process and Docker container."
        support="https://forums.unraid.net/topic/201184-plugin-port-viewer/">

<CHANGES>
A clean, scannable view of every TCP/UDP port your Unraid box is listening
on, with the owning process and Docker container (when applicable) attributed
to each. Lives under Settings -&gt; Network Services.

Highlights
- TCP + UDP, IPv4 + IPv6.
- Resolves docker-proxy host-port mappings back to their owning container,
  including port ranges and host-network containers.
- Color-codes addresses by exposure so externally reachable listeners pop:
  wildcard (0.0.0.0 / ::) and public addresses get a tint and an edge stripe;
  LAN, link-local and localhost are de-emphasised.
- Collapses identical IPv4/IPv6 pairs and per-interface bindings into one row
  with an expandable +N badge so multi-binding daemons (wsdd2, avahi, etc.)
  don't dominate the list. Same for a service listening on both TCP and UDP
  (e.g. DNS) - one row with combined Proto chips instead of two.
- Well-known port labels (SSH, DNS, HTTP/HTTPS, NFS, SMB, MySQL, ...).
- Filter pills + free-text search; sortable, resizable, reorderable and
  hideable columns with state persisted in localStorage.
- Pure JS / PHP, no external dependencies.
</CHANGES>

<!-- Pre-install cleanup: remove any prior ports packages (defensive against
     the old non-conformant naming scheme that upgradepkg can't merge) and
     clear the install dir so stale .page files from earlier versions don't
     register duplicate menu entries. Safe to re-run. -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
for pkg in /var/log/packages/&name;-*; do
  [ -f "$pkg" ] || continue
  removepkg "$(basename "$pkg")" 2&gt;/dev/null || true
done
rm -rf &emhttp;
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="&plgPATH;/&pkg;.txz">
<URL>&repo;/archive/&pkg;.txz</URL>
<MD5>&md5;</MD5>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="upgradepkg --install-new">
<LOCAL>&plgPATH;/&pkg;.txz</LOCAL>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
for pkg in /var/log/packages/&name;-*; do
  [ -f "$pkg" ] || continue
  removepkg "$(basename "$pkg")" 2&gt;/dev/null || true
done
rm -rf &emhttp;
rm -rf &plgPATH;
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
